coverage=[r for r in availability if r['yc']=='0' and r['variable'] in ['hghschoolgrade','preprim','wi_new','elecq','work_week','type_act']]
sections=[]
def section(title,content): sections.append('<section><h2>'+title+'</h2>'+content+'</section>')
section('Decision and scope', '''<div class="finding"><p><strong>A separate-cross-section employment extension is feasible using the archived raw files plus frozen legacy historical covariates. A fully contemporaneous replication of the old specification, or a new older-cohort cognitive-outcome analysis in both rounds, is not feasible from the phone instruments.</strong></p></div>
<p>The existing R1–R5 sample, treatment construction and results stay on the legacy constructed release. New outcomes should be produced in separate files. “Cross section” means one observation per person at a chosen interview occasion, estimated separately by round; linking historical treatment/background by ID does not require panel estimators or a balanced panel. These are follow-ups of the Young Lives cohort, not independent representative samples of Ethiopia.</p>
<p>This is a materials, schema, coverage and definition audit. It does not yet certify final outcome classifications, completion/consent eligibility or regression sample sizes, and it does not fit new regressions.</p>''')
section('Materials located locally', f'''<p>The inventory successfully reads <strong>{len(inv)} Ethiopian Stata modules</strong> across Round 6 and 7 (excluding contact telephone/email lists) and records their variable labels, value-code metadata and nonmissing counts. It also lists {len(docs)} documentation/archive files across the releases; not all are Ethiopia-specific.</p>
<ul><li><strong>R6 Calls 1–3:</strong> UKDA-8678; main OC/YC call files, household rosters, mental-health item files; questionnaires, manuals, Ethiopia codebooks and technical notes.</li>
<li><strong>R6 Calls 4–5:</strong> UKDA-9008; main OC/YC call files, household rosters and supplementary modules; call-specific questionnaires, manuals, Ethiopia codebooks and consent materials.</li>
<li><strong>R7:</strong> UKDA-9538; OC/YC individual and household files, separate phone files, education-history, languages, migration, training, children and ACASI modules. Full and abbreviated Amhara questionnaires, fieldwork/manual and attrition reports are present.</li>
<li><strong>Constructed release:</strong> UKDA-9543 and TN63 offer metadata and a reference panel. They are useful cross-checks but are not a reliable sole source for R6 sample flags or all historical controls. The updated construction do-file remains unavailable locally.</li>
<li><strong>Legacy dependencies:</strong> the original constructed file and current four analysis CSVs are present. Their frozen treatment, instrument and historic controls can be linked without rebuilding or replacing the R1–R5 analysis.</li></ul>
<p>Most required materials are therefore on hand for a raw-data employment extension. What is not yet available is a validated harmonization program for these new outcomes; some exact old measures were never collected in the new modes.</p>''')
section('Which Round 6 observation?', '''<p><strong>Recommend Call 2 as the primary R6 cross section (August–October 2020), conditional on eligibility checks.</strong> It has a directly administered employment battery and greater coverage than Calls 3 or 5. Treat Call 5 (October–November 2021) as a separately labelled sensitivity, not a source from which to mix whichever answers happen to be present for each person.</p>
<p>Call 1 and Call 4 do not supply the same detailed current main-activity employment battery. Call 3 and Call 5 use previous-call information and skip repeated work characteristics when unchanged; their sparse raw activity columns cannot be read as unemployed or no activity. Reconstruct prefills under the questionnaire’s confirmation rules, retain the origin call and target date, and flag unresolved cases. This use of prior-call metadata supports one target cross section; it is not pooling five observations per person.</p>
<p>The raw older-cohort Call 2 file has 129 Tigray records; the Call 5 region distribution has none. Call 5 therefore changes the available policy-region support and cannot be treated as the same geographic experiment with merely a later date. Survey timing, COVID restrictions and conflict also limit interpretation of changes between rounds.</p>''')
section('Observed record counts and potential historical-control coverage', table(main_counts,['file','round','call','yc','raw_records','unique_ids','duplicate_ids','baseline_sample_overlap','baseline_original_filter_eligible','baseline_controls_complete','date_min','date_max','date_out_of_window'])+'''
<p><strong>Counts above are raw records and upper bounds before validating new outcomes/consent, not final analytic N.</strong> The baseline for older participants is the union of existing aa_samp.csv and non_aa_samp.csv; eligibility retains their nonzero/nonmissing R4 language-score filter. For younger participants, the baseline is ylsample_yc.csv and its existing no-language-score-filter convention; the caregiver-sex control is omitted as in the current YC models. Cohorts are not pooled.</p>
<p>For the older-cohort primary design, Call 2 has 773 records linked to the existing sample and 640 satisfying the original language filter plus fixed IV covariates. R7 has 631 in-person and 64 phone records linked to that sample; the corresponding historical-control ceilings are 520 and 53. These ceilings combine Addis and non-Addis and do not apply the additional Addis-specific entype_r4 requirement. Outcome-specific missingness/exclusions and the unresolved R6 eligibility flags will reduce or change these counts.</p>
<p>Dates are parsed rather than ordered as strings. The table flags three out-of-window dates in the YC Call 1 file; Call 4 dates are also inventoried, although that call lacks the target employment battery. These issues do not determine the recommended Call 2 choice.</p>''')
section('Two archive discrepancies that must be resolved', '''<div class="finding"><p><strong>Do not use the new constructed R6 incall flag as the sole eligibility filter.</strong> For Call 2 OC, all 774 raw IDs match original cohort IDs, but 182 are labelled incall=0/inround=0 in the constructed file despite status “Interview completed.” Of these, 181 have CNSNT1COV2=1 and the remaining record has a different consent route; the annual employment screen is populated in the raw file. Similar raw-presence/constructed-flag discrepancies occur in the other calls.</p></div>
<p>The discrepancy is established; its cause is not. Compare raw consent branches, completion/end-of-interview information and official codebooks, then ask the depositors if a documented exclusion rule cannot explain it. Do not automatically include all raw rows either: key presence, consent and completed usable interview are distinct conditions. Record competing eligibility counts until resolved.</p>
<p>R7 raw files and the constructed release agree on <strong>2,038 in-person plus 193 phone records</strong> (OC 632+64; YC 1,406+129), with 2,231 indicated interviewed in the constructed file. The attrition report’s table gives 2,231, while its prose and TN63 describe 205 phone interviews; 2,038+205 would be 2,243. The 12-case discrepancy needs clarification. Use the observed archived-file mode counts for this inventory and never invent or impute absent respondents.</p>''')
section('Variable-by-variable feasibility and construction plan',table([dict(zip(['block','legacy','round6_source','round7_source','feasibility','construction_plan'],r)) for r in crosswalk]))
section('The important employment comparability decision', '''<p>The current outcome uses the first listed R5 annual activity, whereas the R6 instrument asks about the most-time paid or unpaid activity in the past week. R7 contains past-week main-activity fields in both interview modes and past-year main-activity detail in person. Phone R7 has annual participation screens, but no corresponding typwrk12r7 activity-type field in the inspected core file. An annual participation indicator cannot recreate annual wage/salary classification.</p>
<p>Prepare distinct named outcomes rather than a single label that hides these differences:</p><ol>
<li><strong>Common R6/R7 weekly outcomes:</strong> worked last week; employed including temporary absence; main weekly activity categories; salary and wage indicators with explicit denominators. In R7 combine farm, non-household and own-business screening routes before classifying nonworkers.</li>
<li><strong>Legacy-category sensitivity:</strong> preserve salary code 13 and wage codes 5/12/13, along with the original nonworker and competing-activity exclusions where a defensible weekly analogue exists. Keep a conventional all-eligible-respondent denominator as a separately named outcome, not an unnoticed change.</li>
<li><strong>R7 annual bridge:</strong> compare in-person past-year main-activity results with legacy annual results, acknowledging first-listing versus most-time selection and mode exclusions. This is a closer bridge, not demonstrated exact equivalence.</li></ol>
<p>Negative values, 77/79/88 and blank entries must be classified using source-specific labels and routing. Set nonwork to zero only when a valid screen establishes it; distinguish not asked, no matching module, refusal, unknown and inconsistent responses. Public-sector employment can be classified using employer code labels in both rounds, rather than inferred from salary status.</p>''')
section('Why the old full contemporary specification cannot simply be copied', '''<p>R6 did not measure childhood anthropometric z-scores or reproduce the R1 caregiver module, and it did not collect everything needed for the original full wealth index. The new constructed R6 hghschoolgrade, preprim, wi_new and elecq fields are empty even though some raw phone education/electricity responses exist. Adult R7 anthropometry and current household measures do not have the same meaning as childhood controls.</p>
<p><strong>Feasible main specification:</strong> retain historical IMTI/E_is, baseline nutrition, caregiver characteristics, R3 wealth/electricity/household size/food situation and R1 land variables exactly as used previously. Interpret the new regressions as later employment associated with historical exposure and background. Contemporaneous household variables may be added in explicitly separate specifications; they can be affected by treatment and are not automatic substitutes for baseline controls.</p>
<p>Freeze older-cohort MTI and policy assignment for comparability. A new, fully updated MTI variable requires a separate reconstruction of completed schooling and instruction language; R6’s highest-completed question is skipped for some currently enrolled respondents, and R7 qualification and course codes are not years. In the younger cohort, freezing R5 exposure at age roughly 15 can omit subsequent eligible schooling; estimate and interpret that cohort separately until its exposure construction is validated.</p>
<p>There is no common fresh OC maths/verbal outcome in both new rounds. R7’s in-person younger-cohort reading/RACER modules can support a distinct analysis, not a direct continuation of the existing OC cognitive outcomes. Do not treat the lack of a new test as a reason to require a nonexistent new language-score field.</p>'''+table(coverage,['round','call','yc','records','variable','observed'])+'''
<p>The availability counts are nonmissing entries, not validated values or counts of employed people. Constructed status flags themselves have the discrepancies described above.</p>''')
section('Geography, modes, selection and inference', '''<ul>
<li>Decode region separately in every source. For example, legacy region 1 is Tigray and 14 is Addis; R7 regionr7 uses 1 for Addis, 11 for Tigray, 8 for Oromia and 9 for Sidama. Keep current residence, baseline policy region and workplace region as distinct fields.</li>
<li>R7’s shorter phone survey is concentrated in conflict-affected Amhara sites; mode is not randomly assigned. Include a mode indicator and report regional/mode composition. Compare common weekly outcomes in both modes, with an in-person-only sensitivity; exclude phone records only from modules demonstrably not administered.</li>
<li>Retain the earlier sample eligibility and historical covariates, but do not require interviews in both R6 and R7 or silently restrict to a balanced sample. Show each round’s reachable, consented, linked, outcome-observed and complete-case counts. An intersection sample is a sensitivity check only.</li>
<li>Compare attrition/phone participation by baseline region, sex, schooling, IMTI and outcome history. No validated attrition/cross-section weighting construction was established in this inventory; consult the attrition materials before considering weighting. Do not describe these cohort follow-ups as national cross sections.</li>
<li>Recompute first-stage diagnostics on each exact estimation sample and retain the few-policy-region inference approach. Extra calls or Sidama’s new administrative status do not supply independent historical policy clusters. Formal tests comparing rounds must account for repeated individuals even if the regressions themselves are separate cross sections.</li></ul>''')
section('Feasible implementation sequence and acceptance criteria', '''<ol>
<li><strong>Lock the reference:</strong> retain the legacy catalog entry, current four CSVs and baseline model snapshots. Store extension scripts/results separately; disable Overleaf exports while developing.</li>
<li><strong>Resolve eligibility first:</strong> reconcile R6 raw-versus-constructed flags; validate consent branches, unique IDs and dates. Clarify the R7 193/205 phone count. Select Call 2 in advance for the primary R6 estimate, with Call 5 explicitly separate.</li>
<li><strong>Build a frozen historical lookup:</strong> one row per existing participant containing cohort, historic region, IMTI/E_is and all original covariates. Include original eligibility flags and variable-source dates. Existing raw-language eligibility remains a historical choice, not a new outcome screen.</li>
<li><strong>Construct outcomes in isolation:</strong> validate weekly screens, temporary absences, activity and employer code crosswalks; apply confirmed prefill rules where relevant. Produce provisional r6_call2 and r7 cross-section datasets, one row per eligible ID, retaining a R7 mode flag and a source/missingness ledger.</li>
<li><strong>Validate before fitting:</strong> compare raw/constructed counts without assuming constructed flags are authoritative; check unique keys, disjoint modes, working-status/activity consistency, salary⊆wage for matched denominators, source-specific special codes and a row-by-row missingness waterfall. Manually inspect a small authorized sample of ambiguous routed cases locally without exporting identifiers in reports.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate separate cross sections:</strong> older cohort primary, same historical treatment/control specification, exact-sample first stages, regional inference and sample composition tables. Add alternative denominators, R7 annual bridge, in-person-only R7, Call 5 and common-person sensitivities. YC and public/private mechanisms are separate extensions.</li>
<li><strong>Review before migration:</strong> require zero unexplained duplicate IDs, documented consent eligibility, an explicit recall-window statement, a signed-off variable crosswalk, and unchanged legacy reference checks. Only then consider manuscript tables; this report does not authorize replacing their current contents.</li></ol>
<p>The first implementation deliverable should be descriptive cross-section build/audit outputs, not a regression table assembled by filling unavailable variables. No additional data purchase or download is required for that initial stage. Exact annual comparability for R6 and a common contemporaneous cognitive battery cannot be obtained merely by finding more filenames in the supplied archives.</p>''')
